# Cursor Rules - AI Image Editor

## Tech Stack
- **Package Manager**: pnpm (NOT npm/yarn)
- **Framework**: Next.js 15 with App Router
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS v4
- **UI Components**: shadcn/ui (install with pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add)
- **AI SDK**: Vercel AI SDK v5 (@ai-sdk/openai)
- **TypeScript**: Strict mode enabled

## Installation Commands
- Dependencies: `pnpm add [package]`
- shadcn components: `pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add [component]`
- Dev dependencies: `pnpm add -D [package]`

## File Structure
```
app/
├── api/edit-image/route.ts     # Vercel AI SDK integration
├── page.tsx                    # Main page with ImageEditor
└── layout.tsx                  # Root layout
components/
├── ui/                         # shadcn components
├── ImageUpload.tsx            # File upload component
├── PromptInput.tsx            # Text input component
├── GeneratedImage.tsx         # Result display component
└── ImageEditor.tsx            # Main orchestrator
```

## Component Guidelines
- Use TypeScript interfaces for all props
- Implement proper loading states
- Add error boundaries and validation
- Use shadcn/ui components for consistent design
- Follow React best practices (hooks, memo when needed)

## API Integration
- Use Vercel AI SDK v5's generateImage function
- Handle image uploads as base64
- Implement proper error handling
- Use environment variables for API keys

## Styling Rules
- Use Tailwind CSS v4 classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design
- Use shadcn/ui design tokens
- Implement proper hover/focus states

## State Management
- Use React useState for component state
- Lift state up to ImageEditor component
- Implement proper state flow: upload → prompt → generate → display

## Error Handling
- Validate file types and sizes
- Handle API errors gracefully
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Implement retry mechanisms

## Security
- Validate uploads on both client and server
- Sanitize user inputs
- Keep API keys in environment variables
- Implement rate limiting considerations

## Code Quality
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Follow ESLint and Prettier configurations
- Write descriptive variable and function names
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Implement proper prop validation

## Performance
- Optimize image handling (compression, format conversion)
- Implement proper loading states
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Lazy load components when appropriate

## User Experience
- Provide clear feedback for all user actions
- Implement proper loading indicators
- Handle edge cases gracefully
- Ensure responsive design across devices
- Add keyboard navigation support
